# Migrations

A change to a stored table is an Alembic revision. Autogenerate compares the connected database with `Base.metadata` from `app.db` and writes a candidate in `app/schema/versions`. The candidate is not the migration until it has been reviewed and corrected. [Autogenerate](https://alembic.sqlalchemy.org/en/latest/autogenerate.html) does not see every change.

Generate it with `revision --autogenerate` (`command.revision(..., autogenerate=True)`). The config is the one in `app.schema`: `script_location` is that package, and the engine is `config.attributes["engine"]`. `env.py` passes `target_metadata=Base.metadata`. The revision id is `YYYYMMDD_slug`, and `down_revision` is the current head. `apply_schema` upgrades to that head. Stored rows stay.

Autogenerate detects added and removed tables and columns, nullable changes, named unique constraints, indexes, and foreign keys. Review a type change. A renamed table or column arrives as a drop plus an add and must be rewritten as a rename. Name every constraint, or it is skipped. Write a data move by hand between the schema operations. A candidate that drops a table this change did not remove is wrong. Leave the generated downgrade only after it no longer destroys rows this change must keep.

```python
# Bad: a renamed column left as a drop and an add
op.drop_column("campaigns", "name")
op.add_column("campaigns", Column("title", Text, nullable=False))

# Good: the candidate was edited into a rename
op.alter_column("campaigns", "name", new_column_name="title")
```
